  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1127 AM CST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nDry weather is set to continue through Friday as a fairly large-\namplitude upper ridge axis shifts overhead in advance of our next \nstorm system. This will keep temperatures on the mild side, but a \nreturn to southerly surface flow will also result in a steadily \nmoistening low/midlevel atmosphere heading into the weekend. On \nSaturday, models remain in good agreement depicting a relatively \nbroad midlevel disturbance lifting over west-central TX in advance \nof a more potent shortwave trough over the Four Corners. This first \nwave will likely bring a few showers to the region on Saturday, but \nit is the second wave which is expected to bring a more widespread \nround of showers Saturday night into early Sunday. This second round \nwill likely bring the highest rain totals to areas off the Caprock \nbut amounts through the entire event are nevertheless expected to be \nrelatively light, with totals currently expected to be around a half \ninch or less through Sunday morning. A period of drier weather will \nthen commence from Monday through about midweek as weak upper \nridging builds overhead. However, temperatures are expected to be on \nthe cool side early in the week with highs falling into the 50s Mon-\nTue.",
